## Java Production Code Rules

### Dependency Injection
- ALWAYS use constructor injection. Never `@Autowired` on fields.
- Declare injected fields as `private final`.

```java
// ✅ Correct
@Service
public class OrderService {
    private final OrderRepository orderRepository;
    private final NotificationService notificationService;

    public OrderService(OrderRepository orderRepository,
                        NotificationService notificationService) {
        this.orderRepository = orderRepository;
        this.notificationService = notificationService;
    }
}

// ❌ Wrong — never do this
@Autowired
private OrderRepository orderRepository;
```

### Null Safety
- Never return `null` from a service method. Use `Optional<T>` or throw a domain exception.
- Annotate all parameters and return types with `@NonNull` / `@Nullable` where ambiguous.

### Logging
- Use `@Slf4j` (Lombok). Never `System.out.println`.
- Log at `DEBUG` for method entry/exit in service layer.
- Log at `WARN` for recoverable errors, `ERROR` only for unrecoverable failures.

### Exception Handling
- Define domain exceptions extending `RuntimeException` per module.
- Use `@RestControllerAdvice` for a global handler — never catch-and-swallow in services.

### Validation
- Annotate request DTOs with Bean Validation (`@NotNull`, `@NotBlank`, `@Size`, etc.)
- Add `@Validated` to the controller class.

### FeignClient / External Calls
- All FeignClient interfaces must have a `@CircuitBreaker` or `@Retry` annotation.
- Always provide a fallback method for circuit breakers.

### Transactions
- `@Transactional` on service methods that write to the database.
- Read-only queries: `@Transactional(readOnly = true)`.

