## Groovy / Spock Test Rules

### Test File Location
- Unit specs: `src/test/groovy/[package]/[layer]/[ClassName]Spec.groovy`
- Integration specs: `src/test/groovy/[package]/integration/[ClassName]IntegrationSpec.groovy`

### Spec Structure
- Every spec extends `spock.lang.Specification`.
- Use `@Subject` to annotate the class under test.
- Use Spock `Mock()` and `Stub()` — never Mockito inside Spock specs.
- All interaction verifications go in `then:` blocks, not `setup:`.
- End every interaction block with `0 * _` to assert no unexpected interactions.

### Naming Convention
- Method name describes behaviour: `"should [expected] when [condition]"`
- Use `@Unroll` for data-driven tests with `where:` tables.

### Coverage Requirements
- Every new service class must have a corresponding `*Spec.groovy`.
- Every new controller endpoint must have at least one `MockMvc`-based spec.
- Every exception/failure branch must have a dedicated test block.
- Minimum: line coverage ≥ 80%, branch coverage ≥ 75% (enforced by JaCoCo Maven plugin).

### What NOT to do in Spock
```groovy
// ❌ Never use Mockito annotations in a Spock spec
@Mock
OrderRepository repository

// ❌ Never use verify() from Mockito
verify(service).doSomething()

// ✅ Always use Spock interaction syntax
1 * repository.findById(_) >> Optional.of(order)
```

