## Feature Flag Rules — Azure App Configuration (enforced on every new feature)

### When a Feature Flag Is Required
A feature flag is MANDATORY whenever a story introduces:
- A new API endpoint (any HTTP method)
- A new Kafka producer or consumer
- A change to an existing business rule or calculation
- Any integration with a new external service or DB table

A feature flag is NOT required for:
- Pure refactoring (no behaviour change visible to callers)
- Test-only changes
- Logging, metrics, or observability additions only

### Naming Convention
```
feature.[domain].[story-id]-[short-description]
```
Examples:
- `feature.order.proj-123-express-checkout`
- `feature.payment.proj-456-new-gateway`
- `feature.notification.proj-789-push-alerts`

### Every Flag Must Be Declared in FeatureFlags.java
```java
// FeatureFlags.java — central registry, one constant per flag
public static final String ORDER_EXPRESS_CHECKOUT =
    "feature.order.proj-123-express-checkout";
```
Never use a raw string literal for a flag key in production code.

### Default State: ALWAYS Disabled
Every new flag must default to `false` (disabled) in:
1. `application.yml` local dev fallback: `feature.[domain].[key]: false`
2. Azure App Configuration (Terraform/Bicep): `enabled = false`

### Guard Pattern: New Code Is Never Reachable Without Explicit Enable
```java
// ✅ Correct — guard at top of method, before any new logic
if (!featureFlagService.isEnabled(FeatureFlags.ORDER_EXPRESS_CHECKOUT)) {
    log.warn("Feature disabled — returning existing behaviour. flag={}",
        FeatureFlags.ORDER_EXPRESS_CHECKOUT);
    return existingBehaviour();      // existing path, unchanged
}
// new implementation below here only
```

### Kill-Switch Behaviour Contract
When flag is disabled at runtime:
- Service methods: return existing code path (no new behaviour)
- New-only endpoints (no existing path): return `404 Not Found`
- Kafka listeners: `ack.acknowledge()` + discard (never dead-letter for a disabled flag)
- Log at `WARN`: `"Feature disabled — [behaviour]. flag=[key]"`
- Increment counter: `meterRegistry.counter("[domain].[feature].flag-disabled").increment()`

### Azure App Configuration Caching
- Flag evaluation MUST use the local cached value — NOT a live Azure call per request
- `bootstrap.yml` must have: `refresh.interval: 30s` (minimum)
- Never call `featureManager.isEnabled()` inside a loop
- Flag state refreshes across all pods within the refresh interval — no restart needed

### What Cascade Must NEVER Do
- Never write new feature code that is reachable without a feature flag guard
- Never hardcode a flag key as a raw string — always use `FeatureFlags.[CONSTANT]`
- Never default a new flag to `true` — always `false`
- Never evaluate a feature flag inside a `@Transactional` method or a loop
- Never create a flag without a kill-switch runbook in the feature flag report

