## Scalability & Architecture Rules (enforced when writing any new API or service)

### The Core Rule
Before writing any new class, controller, or service method for a new feature,
Cascade MUST check whether an ADR exists for this story:
```bash
ls docs/adr/ADR-[STORY-ID]-*.md
```
If no ADR exists, STOP and run `/architecture-review` before writing a single line.

---

### Reactive vs Blocking — When to Use Each

**Use Spring WebFlux (Mono/Flux) when:**
- The service makes 2+ downstream calls that can run in parallel
- Expected concurrency > 500 simultaneous connections
- The service is primarily I/O bound (waiting on DB, APIs, Kafka)
- Streaming responses are required (SSE, chunked)

**Use Spring MVC (blocking) when:**
- The team has limited reactive experience AND throughput is < 500 req/s
- The service is primarily compute-bound (PDF generation, complex calculations)
- All downstream calls are synchronous and cannot be parallelised
- The ADR explicitly chose MVC after evaluating both options

**NEVER mix reactive and blocking on the same thread:**
```java
// ❌ DEADLY — blocks the reactive event loop thread
public Mono<Order> getOrder(String id) {
    Order order = orderRepository.findById(id).get();  // blocking JPA on reactive thread
    return Mono.just(order);
}

// ✅ Correct — use R2DBC for reactive DB access
public Mono<Order> getOrder(String id) {
    return reactiveOrderRepository.findById(id);  // R2DBC — non-blocking
}

// ✅ Correct — if JPA is required, wrap in boundedElastic scheduler
public Mono<Order> getOrder(String id) {
    return Mono.fromCallable(() -> orderRepository.findById(id).orElseThrow())
        .subscribeOn(Schedulers.boundedElastic());
}
```

