### Scalability Design Rules

**Thread pool sizing — never use defaults blindly:**
- Tomcat (MVC): default 200 threads — at 200ms avg response time → 1,000 req/s max
- Netty (WebFlux): 2× CPU cores event threads → handles thousands of concurrent connections
- Kafka consumers: one thread per partition — size consumer group accordingly
- Resilience4j Bulkhead: size per downstream, not per service

**Memory pressure rules:**
- Never load unbounded collections into memory — always paginate (page size ≤ 100)
- Never hold DB connections during external API calls
- Use `Flux.fromIterable()` + `flatMap(concurrency)` for parallel processing of lists:
  ```java
  // Process up to 10 items concurrently, not all at once
  Flux.fromIterable(orderIds)
      .flatMap(id -> pricingClient.getPrice(id), 10)  // concurrency = 10
      .collectList()
  ```

**Caching rules:**
- Cache reads that are stable for > 1 minute: `@Cacheable(value="prices", key="#productId")`
- Always set TTL on cache entries — no infinite caches
- Cache stampede: use `@CachePut` on writes, not just `@Cacheable` on reads
- For reactive caching: `Mono.fromCallable(...).cache(Duration.ofMinutes(5))`

